<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mamprugu</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1898 CE (approx.) · capital Nalerigu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior kingdom of the Mole-Dagbani peoples of northern Ghana, from whose royal house the Dagbon and Mossi dynasties claim descent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mole-dagbani">Mole-Dagbani</a> tradition traces Mamprugu to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-gbewaa">Na Gbewaa</a>, a chief who settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pusiga">Pusiga</a> in the far north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, probably in the 14th or 15th century. His sons and grandsons are said to have founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dagbon">Dagbon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanumba">Nanumba</a> and, through the princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms, so the Nayiri of Mamprugu is regarded as their senior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A ruling class of horse-owning chiefs governed farming peoples whose earth-priests kept authority over land and shrines. The capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gambaga">Gambaga</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalerigu">Nalerigu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/na-atabia">Na Atabia</a> around 1700. Muslim traders and scholars settled at Gambaga, linking the kingdom to routes between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> forest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mamprugu was divided in 1898 between the British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a>, with a smaller part in French territory. The Nayiri still reigns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalerigu">Nalerigu</a> as paramount chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamprusi">Mamprusi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1300 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:11.90%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1898 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nalerigu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Na Gbewaa, Na Atabia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mampruli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a href="/countries/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Mamprugu, Mamprusi kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1599&amp;focus=mamprugu" aria-label="Mamprugu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4670 1785 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mamprugu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4990 2041L4990 1994M4990 2041L4972 2079M4990 2041L5000 2081"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4990 1994h0M4972 2079h0M5000 2081h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 2041h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 2041h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nalerigu. <a href="/atlas?year=1599&amp;focus=mamprugu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
